.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.pem
*.key

# --- Data: large, regenerable, not ours to redistribute ---------------------
backend/data/raw/
backend/data/*.duckdb
backend/data/*.duckdb.wal

# ...except the serving database, which is committed deliberately.
#
# 1.3 MB holding 1,324 aggregate medians - one per (area, property kind,
# bedrooms) cell - not the 9.8M individual contracts it was computed from. The
# build database beside it stays out at 360 MB.
#
# A derived artifact in source control normally goes stale silently. This one
# cannot: it carries its own `snapshot_id`, /healthz publishes the age of the
# newest contract in it, and past MARKET_SNAPSHOT_MAX_AGE_DAYS the service
# quotes nothing at all rather than quoting something old.
#
# It is committed because our host builds from the repository, and without it
# the deployed service reports `degraded` and offers manual entry only - which
# is a supported state, but not the thing the product is for (D-102).
!backend/data/comparables.duckdb

# The case store, and the two files SQLite keeps beside it. This holds consent
# records, case rows and the full body of every evidence pack — operational data
# about real people. The patterns above covered DuckDB and JSON Lines because
# those were the formats at the time; SQLite arrived later and was committed
# before anyone noticed, which is exactly why the test below exists.
backend/data/*.db
backend/data/*.db-wal
backend/data/*.db-shm
backend/data/*.sqlite
backend/data/*.sqlite3

# The audit log holds caller-stated facts - rents, dates, areas. It is real
# operational data about real people and must never reach the repository.
backend/data/*.jsonl
backend/data/audit.jsonl
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

